 "badge": {
  "id": "battle-for-the-airfields",
  "name": "Battle for the Airfields"
 },
 "briefing": {
  "situation": "Dawn, 7 December. Japanese fighters are crossing the western hills toward Tacloban, with bombers and troop transports close behind them, bound for the Burauen airfields. You are a P-38 pilot on the ramp at Tacloban with bombs and rockets. Stop at least five of the ten bombers and transports before they release, keep at least two of Buri, Bayug and San Pablo serviceable until the relief force arrives, then destroy six vehicles of the Japanese lodgment and land at Tacloban, Bayug or Buri.",
  "loadout": "both"
 },
 "history": {
  "text": "On 6 and 7 December 1944 the Japanese struck the Leyte airfields with fighters, bombers and paratroop transports (Operation Te-Go), and Fifth Air Force fighters shot down dozens of them. The Americans held all three Burauen fields. This sortie compresses the air fighting and the ground action of both days into one dawn, and its numbers are a playable finale: the exact raids, the Ki-57 as the transport (the source does not name every transport) and the Type B Marus standing in for American transports are representative, not a reconstruction of one recorded mission.",
  "sources": [
   "M. Hamlin Cannon, Leyte: The Return to the Philippines (Washington: Office of the Chief of Military History, 1954; CMH Pub 5-9-1), ch. XVII, U.S. Army Center of Military History: https://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Return/USA-P-Return-17.html",
   "United States Strategic Bombing Survey, The Fifth Air Force in the War Against Japan (1947): https://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/NHC/NewPDFs/USAAF/United%20States%20Strategic%20Bombing%20Survey/USSBS%20Fifth%20Air%20Force%20in%20the%20War%20Against%20Japan.pdf"
  ]
 }
